Audit.67 proves a conditional finite release for one specified curved-wall chamber and the narrowed blocker count: blocked roots require distinct interior source points, which need not be isolates. The C-II frontier is unchanged.

Audit.68 bounds the full interior population of a near-triangular curved unit wall by the exact packet capacity via Oler, then excludes every canonical source in that chamber class. No arbitrary canonical-source extraction is known, so the C-II frontier is unchanged.

Audit.69 extends the wall packet to uneven opposite-side profiles for `M>=8`; a Green-function margin selects the middle bank, and Oler again pays the full old interior. A local concentrated-curvature regression rules out the corner-adjacent root variant. The C-II frontier is unchanged.

Audit.70 rules out the complete triangular-lattice interior packet under all six retained corner guards for near-identity homotheties and C2 affine families. Other packets, corner-guard exchanges, and nonaffine motions are not excluded.

Audit.71 establishes a local necessary condition for any C2 feasible motion of that full limiting grid under the stated six-guard expansion: each nested ring requires squared-velocity energy at least `2M(M^2-1)`, and the selected stresses force `3M(M+1)` first variations to zero. This does not give an energy upper bound or produce a feasible packet.

Audit.72 proves a different finite packet theorem for a specified six-sided curved wall. Omitting the chart center and one actual wall corner destroys the full-grid positive-stress obstruction and yields a nonaffine strict packet with `N-1` sites, `N=3M^2-3M+1`. It pays every wall-interior source population `z<=N-2` by deleting those labels and one corner while retaining all exterior points and losing at least two contacts. Its Oler argument leaves `z=N-1` or `N`; Audits.77--78 subsequently exclude the complete specified fixed-profile wall family for every fixed `M>=4`. No arbitrary wall extraction follows; C-II remains open.

Audit.73 constructs exact sorting packets for every radial-rate permutation. These packets realize equality `E_r=12C` simultaneously for every nested ring, including a center-deleted chart that still contains unit triangles. This rules out the strict-energy-gap route under only the physical unit-lower and six-guard assumptions. It does not establish a stronger triangle-free packet obstruction or a global source conclusion. Audit.78 separately proves the fixed-profile `z=N` classification; arbitrary-wall extraction remains open.

Audit.74 uses the Thue–Groemer equality case to show that any sequence in this wall class with `z=N` interior points converges, after relabeling and passage to a subsequence, to the complete radius-`M` triangular lattice. Equivalently, all sufficiently small-curvature `z=N` placements lie arbitrarily close to `P_(M-1)`. This is limiting localization only; Audit.78 supplies a separate finite positive-curvature classification and triangle conclusion for each fixed profile.

Audit.75 proves a complementary auxiliary-chart fact: for every flat triangular-lattice hexagon of radius `M>=2`, deleting any one interior site while pinning the full boundary leaves a zero static first-order cone and a locally isolated flat chart. Under any `O(theta)` boundary perturbation, feasible placements converging to this vacancy chart move by only `O(theta)` and have bounded first velocities. The theorem neither extracts such a chart from an arbitrary source nor classifies the resulting curved-wall velocity polytope; it supplies no finite exchange. C-II remains open.

Audit.77 promotes the all-fixed-profile `M>=4` strict one-deficit packet and its full-source `z<=N-1` exclusion for every sufficiently small positive curvature. Audit.78 classifies the remaining `z=N` cloud for every fixed `M>=3` as a sorting packet containing an actual unit triangle. Together with the finite Oler cap this exhausts the specified fixed-wall class for `M>=4`. For `M=3`, the top population is classified but lower populations are not excluded by Audit.77. Audit.76's five exact-curvature certificates remain useful finite checks. Arbitrary-wall extraction and C-II closure remain open.

Audit.79 registers the exact full moving-column rank of the sorting packet contact matrix and the explicit principal radial-chain stress correction for every fixed descending symmetric profile. The weak-row correction is bounded below by `(2/sqrt(3))*min_h(a_(h-1)-a_h)`. This strengthens the local stress bridge only; it supplies no onset uniform in M/profile and does not extract the fixed wall from an arbitrary source.

Audit.80 proves a joint arithmetic-profile window for every `M>=4` and `0<theta<=1/(100*M^3)`: the one-deficit strict packet exists, Oler gives `z<=N`, and a canonical source with `z<=N-1` is excluded while retaining exterior points and contact isolates. The profile is exactly `a_h=M-1-2h`; the opening amplitude is not uniform. Audit.80 itself gives no uniform `z=N` classification or arbitrary-wall extraction; Audit.107 later classifies this specified arithmetic wall on the narrower common interval `0<theta<=c_*M^-40`.

Audit.81 proves that any fixed C2 convex six-sided unit-wall family tending to `H_M` has the same Oler cap `z<=N` near zero curvature. If it realizes `z=N` for arbitrarily small curvature, all six strictly ordered first angular-rate lists must agree after a common infinitesimal rotation; otherwise its maximum population is at most `N-1` below a family-dependent onset. The full-chart pinning rate supplies the required first-order localization. This adds no packet for `z<=N-1`, controls no higher wall coefficients, and does not extract an arbitrary wall from a canonical source.

Audit.82 proves a general low-slack chart theorem: if a unit-wall packing has `sigma=area(F)-(sqrt(3)/4)*(2z+b-2)<=1/1000`, its full Delaunay triangulation develops to a convex triangular-lattice polygon with explicit `60*b*sigma` position error. For the arithmetic six-wall, `M>=3` and `0<theta<=1/(10000*M^3)`, every feasible `z=N` cloud develops to `H_M` and lies within `1000*M^4*theta` of `P_M`. Audit.82 itself is a localization theorem; Audit.107 later gives an exact classification only for the specified arithmetic wall on a narrower interval. Neither result supplies arbitrary-source wall extraction.

Audit.153 promotes `C-ONE-DEFICIT-DIFFUSE-STRAIN-NONGLOBAL-REGRESSION`. For every `M>=3`, an oblique parallelogram grid has exactly one point's area-form Oler allowance over its interior population, no one-site insertion at fixed coordinates, and valence-six Delaunay interiors with triangle excess tending to zero. A same-cardinality square grid has strictly smaller diameter. This nonglobal example blocks treating the Oler allowance or local near-equilateral saturation as a source-level vacancy or optimality certificate; it does not alter the curved-wall theorem scopes or the C-II endpoint.

Audit.83 gives an exact but conditional quarter-fold chain. Face-coupled train signs integrate to a finite contact-preserving fold; nonaffinity yields strict weighted old-diameter decrease on the canonical core for `D>=9`. If every old contact stays unit, every old noncontact stays strictly above one, and all old diameter/nondiameter upper bounds hold in the full source, the endpoint contradicts the lexicographic minimum. The source-realized compatibility graph and convex-face transition theorem sharply restrict such signs. Hexagon and rhombic-grid regressions show the two missing checks: lower feasibility and newly active inactive diameter pairs. No universally feasible nonaffine fold is established; the live obligation remains an endpoint-existence theorem or a different finite move.

Audit.84 promotes the convex hull-augmented cap-cell structure, a one-unit-side shield for a loaded hull tip that checks retained exterior isolates, and the strict capacity identity `Pack_1(E_Q)=|S_Q|` for one actual cell. It also proves actual loaded tips are acute and that low-angle deleted guards add no strict capacity. These are exact single-cell bounds: they do not supply a spare site or packet, prove cross-cell gluing, or extract/eliminate a macroscopic exterior cap. Exact regressions show shared loaded tips can block simultaneous gluing even when each one-at-a-time replacement is sub-D. The C-II endpoint and its face-coupled finite-move/macroscopic-container obligations remain unchanged. See the [Audit.84 report](../../audits/target_c_exterior_hull_cell_and_tip_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.85 proves the internal two-unit-side shield and full-source / cross-cell shields for two actual cells with four distinct extreme loaded tips pairwise at least four apart. In that explicit scope, every locally feasible packet pair is strictly separated across cells; the exact contact/diameter endpoint rules out a full-size exchange with new contact count no larger than the old lost count. Consequently the sum of strict guarded capacities is at most `|S_1|+|S_2|+3`. The cap-pair argument that would derive four-tip separation from macroscopic asymptotics remains unaudited, and no packet population or arbitrary-cap elimination is supplied. The C-II endpoint is unchanged. See the [Audit.85 report](../../audits/target_c_exterior_paired_cell_gluing_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.86 promotes the finite positive-circuit transversal characterization for strict-negative contact directions and records two exact covector regressions. Its source report's residual-cage packet endpoint is valid but superseded for the stated strict wall-safe packets: `kappa>=1` means the packet has at least one spare site, already ruled out by the canonical contact-budget endpoint without a source motion. The abstract matching regression shows `s_Z+1` cannot be improved from the listed rank/topology data alone. No packet construction or source-uniform capacity estimate follows. See the [Audit.86 report](../../audits/target_c_residual_cage_release_refill_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.87 promotes the finite KKT strict-motion alternative and the loaded-diameter-support transversal for the canonical full source. Hitting every normalized stress's positive diameter support with a loaded deletion set guarantees a strict first motion on the reduced source, with `kappa_open<=kappa(H,G)<=tau_*<=s_Z+1`. Contact-isolated points remain in the source and inactive; the motion gives no refill or finite-pivot guarantee. The abstract first-order/finite-release regression is scoped to a smooth one-dimensional chart. The exact C-II capacity and finite-competitor obligation is unchanged. See the [Audit.87 report](../../audits/target_c_contact_opening_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.88 promotes `C-NONCONTACT-WALL-STRICT-SHIELD` for simple polygons with side lengths at most `sqrt(3)`: wall-safe sites are strictly separated from every retained old point outside the wall, and packets inside disjoint wall interiors are jointly safe. `C-WALL-SAFE-WINDING-BARRIER` requires an `n`-edge wall containing any wall-safe site to have maximum side length above `2 sin(pi/n)`. Thus any simple pentagon opened from unit sides needs a label displacement greater than `(2 sin(pi/5)-1)/2`. The exact `sqrt(3)`-threshold counterexample is recorded in the [Audit.88 report](../../audits/target_c_noncontact_wall_shield_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md). No source-opening motion or spare-site capacity follows, so the active C-II obligation is unchanged.

Audit.89 promotes the finite physical-face return-depth population theorem and its subquadratic-core macroscopic-face corollary. For every fixed `eta>0`, at most `m/2+o(D)` vertices of one selected area-`Omega(D^2)` bounded physical contact face can stay at distance at least `eta D` from the actual loaded set; the `C-INACTIVE-CHAMBER` budget also gives `m<=13*pi*D`. The finite count uses one face quota and counts vertices in the nonisolated core; contact isolates remain in the source. This rules out a deeply separated majority boundary in this subquadratic-core subcase, but gives no return-length currency, capacity, refill, or finite competitor. See the [Audit.89 report](../../audits/target_c_macroscopic_face_return_depth_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.90 promotes an exact unloaded-vertex stress transversal. A deletion set `U` inside the nonloaded positive-contact core exists exactly when no normalized KKT stress is supported wholly on the loaded endpoints; `|U|<=s_Z+1` and the reduced source has a strict first motion. In the full-core loaded-chord regime, a nonunit loaded-hull side forces this empty loaded-only stress face. If the face is nonempty, every stress on it uses the entire loaded hull contact cycle and its positive diameter support covers every loaded vertex; any prescribed loaded endpoint plus at most `s_Z` unloaded labels kills all stresses. The loaded-chord implication is used only in its canonical full-core near-disk scope, not the container branch. An exact weighted-derivative regression shows that the deleted unloaded contacts cannot all open along the same reduced descent. No packet, finite activation, or closure follows. See the [Audit.90 report](../../audits/target_c_unloaded_stress_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.91 promotes a uniform affine-lattice extreme-vertex count and three conditional loaded-boundary consequences. Exact-lattice unloaded-component attachments account for at most `O(D^(8/9))` loaded endpoints; therefore a linear remainder must be unused or attached to components without a uniformly conditioned exact lattice. A fixed number of uniformly conditioned affine lattices with `o(1)` physical error cannot cover all but `o(D)` loaded endpoints in the near-disk full-core branch, and a separate diameter-normal bound excludes that cover in either branch. The latter uses every actual diameter edge. No canonical phase theorem supplies this positional cover, and the C-II finite-move/capacity endpoint is unchanged. See the [Audit.91 report](../../audits/target_c_loaded_attachment_lattice_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.92 independently audits the bent-grid positional-transport regression. For every even `M>=8`, a triangle-free `M`-by-`M` rhombic unit-contact grain has phase span below `1/M`, total angular and area excess `O(M)`, positive pinned equilibrium, and full pinned rank, yet its bottom row stays a fixed positive distance from every uniformly conditioned single affine lattice. The boundary pins are not certified actual diameter endpoints; the result does not refute a cover of the canonical loaded set or a bounded multiple-lattice cover. Do not infer single-lattice physical transport from those reduced inputs. See the [Audit.92 report](../../audits/target_c_bent_grid_positional_transport_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.93 independently audits the pentagon-capped locked-bank regression. A near-triangular parallelogram grid can carry `Omega(D)` long actual stopped trains, `Omega(D^2)` crossings, disjoint terminal pentagons with five reduced side phases, positive pinned grid-interior equilibrium, and full pinned rank. Yet exact finite Oler capacity forces every aggregate wall-safe packet bank, including arbitrary composite contact cycles, to have capacity at most its deleted interior population. The pins are not certified actual diameter loads and the source is not globally optimal. This blocks the geometry-only fixed-wall surplus inference; actual load coupling or movable walls remain necessary. See the [Audit.93 report](../../audits/target_c_pentagon_capped_locked_train_bank_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.94 independently audits `C-PAIRED-CAP-SPOKE-NONGLOBAL-REGRESSION`. A finite six-spoke source has actual loaded diameter endpoints and positive KKT equilibrium, antipodal exterior caps each with `Theta(D^2)` isolate population and only `O(D)` Oler allowance, and a `3/5` isolate cover of every depth-one cap point. An exact same-cardinality lattice competitor has diameter `D-1/2` for every `M>=20`. The source fails canonical mixed-rigidity and linear loaded-diameter density, so this excludes inference from the reduced cap features alone; canonical antipodal-cap extraction remains open. See the [Audit.94 report](../../audits/target_c_paired_cap_spoke_global_comparison_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.95 records a non-Horn closure relation for one regular-hexagon face with six independent side bits, plus a seven-point upper-check regression where a frozen contact isolate becomes longer than the old core diameter under the alternating common-angle move. These are finite local guardrails only; they do not classify general train networks or give a canonical feasible motion. See the [Audit.95 report](../../audits/target_c_common_angle_hexagon_and_isolate_regressions_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.96 independently audits `C-CAPPED-WALL-ACTUAL-DIAMETER-ESCAPE-REGRESSION`. For an entire source made from an oblique unit grid and outward regular pentagon caps/leaves with the exact listed contact set, the actual unique diameter is the opposite grid-corner pair; a small common-angle motion of the full packet preserves contacts, all lower bounds, and strictly decreases its actual diameter. Two finite triangle-free instances pass exhaustive interval verification. This blocks replacing the actual upper-bound test with fixed wall pins in this packet class. It does not extend through arbitrary retained points or extract the class from a canonical source; the C-II endpoint is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.96](../../audits/target_c_capped_wall_actual_diameter_escape_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.97 promotes the exact source-dependent amplitude gap `C-COMMON-ANGLE-ACTIVE-DIAMETER-AMPLITUDE-GAP`. For a nonconstant integrated binary train pattern on a canonical C-II core with `D>=9`, the full-support stress forces positive and negative diameter transverse terms. Keeping all old actual diameters at most `D` therefore requires a positive, source-dependent half-angle threshold in either direction (or fails immediately when a required quadratic coefficient has the wrong sign). This does not establish the other full-source pair inequalities, a feasible endpoint, or a uniform threshold; contact isolates remain in the endpoint ledger. The C-II obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.97](../../audits/target_c_common_angle_active_diameter_amplitude_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.98 promotes `C-PENTAGON-TRAIN-COMPONENT-BIT-COLLAPSE`: common-angle closure on an actual physical unit-pentagon face forces all five train bits equal, and this equality propagates throughout each connected pentagon/train component. If such a component has `f>=2` pentagons, reciprocal-train sparsity yields at least `f+6` outside terminal occurrences, all carrying the same component bit. This is a conditional count for an existing component; neither its large-source existence nor linear suppression multiplicity is established. The common-angle full-source endpoint obligation and C-II status do not change. See the [independent Audit.98](../../audits/target_c_pentagon_train_component_bit_collapse_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.99 promotes `C-ZERO-VALID-PINNED-CLOSURE`, an exact path/fork certificate theorem for pinned models of any finite zero-valid 2-CNF formula represented by implications, unary negative clauses, and negative pairs. It gives a compact obstruction whenever a pinned finite-move system has no model. Applying that certificate to canonical source motions still requires the hypotheses of `C-DISJOINT-SWAP-2SAT`; it does not show source geometry excludes the path/fork or prove a shortening move. The Target C and Level I statuses remain open. See the [independent Audit.99](../../audits/target_c_zero_valid_pinned_closure_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.100 promotes four narrow static-anchor results. Under `C=C_*`, `H=H_*`, every isolate has strict distance slack to all other points; finite polynomial avoidance then lets the isolate coordinates move arbitrarily little so no isolate belongs to any old unit-circumradius triple. A pinned singleton therefore cannot use an isolate as a unit anchor. For `D>=10000`, three loaded vertices on a common unit circle form a strictly obtuse triangle, so a pinned one-site replacement must have an unloaded core unit neighbor. Separately, the only weak-clearance site inside an intact simple wall of at most six edges of length at most one is the center of a regular unit hexagon, and insertion creates a triangle. These conditions do not produce a site, packet capacity, or a finite competitor. See the [independent Audit.100](../../audits/target_c_generic_isolate_anchor_avoidance_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.101 promotes `C-FAN-REFLECTION-STRICT-CAPACITY-LOSS-REGRESSION`. For every real `D>=10`, a ten-point triangle-free source with an actual degree-two diameter-fan leaf has a simple eight-edge unit wall whose strict safe interior contains a contact isolate. Reflecting the fan leaf preserves the old wall contacts and the diameter upper bound but collides with the isolate; moreover, six triangles of circumradius at most one cover the reflected wall, so its strict safe interior is empty. This is a nonglobal local regression: it blocks transporting old wall capacity from upper nonexpansiveness or old clearance alone, but it does not refute the canonical fan theorem, exclude unrestricted relocation, or change the C-II finite-competitor obligation. Its 8-cycle contact core is not asserted to satisfy canonical full-core optimality hypotheses. See the [independent Audit.101](../../audits/target_c_fan_reflection_capacity_loss_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.102 promotes `C-THIRD-CONTACT-FAN-REFLECTION-COLLISION-REGRESSION`. In a unit-separated finite source of diameter `D>=2`, if a vertex has two unit fan neighbors at diameter distance from a common carrier and an additional unit neighbor, that third neighbor lies strictly inside the carrier/fan-neighbor triangle. The standard fan reflection preserves the two fan contacts and all diameter upper bounds but makes the third contact strictly shorter than one. This validates the degree-two scope of the canonical fan move; it does not extract such vertices from a canonical source or provide a population bound. See the [independent Audit.102](../../audits/target_c_third_fan_contact_reflection_collision_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.103 promotes `C-LOADED-ANCHOR-SPARSE-PACKET-OBSTRUCTION`. In the canonical `D>=9`, `C=C_*`, `H=H_*` source, a full-source equal-cardinality packet that deletes a contact, keeps every inserted-involving distance strictly below `D`, and uses only loaded retained core anchors must create `J(J+2L_D)>=D/4`, where `L_D=sqrt(D*eta_D+eta_D^2)` and `eta_D=3^(1/4)*sqrt(D)`. Thus bounded-contact packets are impossible in the all-loaded shell for large `D`; the exact lower threshold is asymptotic to `D^(1/4)/(8*3^(1/8))`. The analytic finite release moves retained isolates and uses the thin-strip straight-chain structure of positive stresses. Packets touching unloaded core anchors or creating inserted-involving diameter pairs remain open, and no packet capacity or Level I result follows. See the [independent Audit.103](../../audits/target_c_loaded_anchor_sparse_packet_obstruction_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.104 promotes `C-DIAMETER-ENDPOINT-LOCAL-DEGREE-TWO` and `C-NEARBY-DIAMETER-ENDPOINTS-FULL-HULL-SIDE`. For any fixed `1<t<sqrt(2)`, all sufficiently large global minimizers have at most two actual diameter endpoints within `t` of any source point, and any endpoint pair within `t` is consecutive on the full source hull. The bound includes contact-isolated source points and has a threshold depending only on `t`. This is a geometric incidence bound, not an owner-capacity or finite-move theorem. See the [independent Audit.104](../../audits/target_c_loaded_endpoint_local_incidence_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.105 promotes `C-OO-OUTER-ISOLATE-COLLAR-BOUND` for the Type-II near-disk branch. With `x_B=b-q` and `a` the number of nonunit loaded-hull sides, at most `48(x_B+a)` distinct source isolates lie within distance `5/4` of the convex outer pockets or nondegenerate exterior caps. Under the canonical small O--O assignment, every isolate lower-clause endpoint lies in these collars; if `x_B+a=o(D)`, only `o(D)` distinct isolate blockers remain. See the [independent Audit.105](../../audits/target_c_outer_isolate_collar_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.106 promotes `C-OO-LOWER-WITNESS-OWNER-CAPACITY`. For an actual disjoint-owner O--O family whose counted singletons preserve contacts and upper constraints, pass unary rhombus tests, and strictly shorten an old diameter, each owner requires an outer lower witness. The general bound is `k<=2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out)`. In Type II, the Audit.105 collar count sharpens this to `k<=147(x_B+m_B)`, so a linear counted family is impossible if `x_B+m_B=o(D)`. The theorem does not provide the owner family or the low-defect hypothesis; the original-family `2d_H` deletion claim remains unaudited. C-II is open. See the [independent Audit.106](../../audits/target_c_outer_owner_witness_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.107 promotes the uniform growing-`M` arithmetic six-wall maximum-population classification and canonical source exclusion for `M>=4`, `0<theta<=c_*M^-40` with one universal `c_*>0`. It retains all exterior source points and contact isolates, but does not extract an arithmetic wall from an arbitrary source. Audit.108 adds `C-COUPLED-DELAUNAY-CONVEX-CONTAINER-TRANSFER`: a common closed-unit-covering Delaunay completion gives exact Oler, boundary-strip, and triangle-excess budgets for arbitrary convex polygon crops. When `S,p=O(D)`, the strip, excess, and exceptional-vertex counts are `O(D)`. This is a reusable geometric input only; edges of the Delaunay triangulation need not be source contacts, and no source-level movement/payment is established. The live obligation remains a full-source equal-cardinality competitor that preserves every old lower and actual-diameter upper constraint, including all contact isolates.

Audit.110 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-STATIC-PACKET-BARRIER`. In the canonical `D>=9`, `C=C_*`, `H=H_*` source, no equal-cardinality unit-separated packet outside `int(F=conv(V))` with strict upper slack to all packet-involving pairs can replace labels when the deletion removes a contact or covers every old diameter pair. A finite straight motion opens all new contacts, retaining contact isolates as moving variables; the endpoint contradicts contact minimality or globality. Boundary packet points and temporary triangles are allowed. Audit.109's one-site result is a direct special case and is superseded. The theorem creates no packet or interior capacity, and the moving-core full-source endpoint remains open. See the [independent Audit.110](../../audits/target_c_exterior_static_packet_barrier_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.112 adds `C-SEPARATED-PACKET-RELEASE-OBSTRUCTION`. For a triangle-free contact-minimal global minimizer, a finite equal-cardinality packet split into pieces strictly separated from all retained labels can be translated piecewise and then dilated to open every new unit contact, provided distinct pieces already have strict cross-separation and every inserted-involving upper distance is strict. The resulting contact-free packet is impossible if the deletion removes an old contact, without requiring diameter-pair minimality, and is impossible by globality if the deletion covers every old diameter pair. This adds no packet-existence or capacity result, so the C-II endpoint is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.112](../../audits/target_c_separated_packet_release_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.113 promotes `C-ONE-GUARD-EXTERIOR-CAP-SATURATION`: when the positive-contact core lies in the actual loaded endpoint hull, the weakly clear site capacity in one exterior cap, after omitting any one active contact guard, is exactly the cap's existing isolate population. The upper bound allows exact unit anchors because Audit.112 opens them; the lower bound moves the cap isolates a small distance into the full source hull while preserving all strict pair margins. This is a one-guard cap result, not a surplus or paired-cap theorem. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.113](../../audits/target_c_one_guard_exterior_cap_saturation_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.114 promotes `C-OO-ORIGINAL-FAMILY-OWNER-CAPACITY`. In a fixed, subfamily-stable O–O proposal family, at most `2d_H` singleton proposals can fail an upper check on the canonical exceptional long-pair set. Applying the owner-witness count to the others gives `r<=2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out)+2d_H`, or `r<=147(x_B+m_B)+2d_H` in Type II. This counts individually tested proposals; it does not assert they combine into a feasible motion or that a source supplies such a family. See the [independent Audit.114](../../audits/target_c_original_family_outer_owner_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.115 promotes `C-ANTIPODAL-EXTERIOR-CAP-PAIRING`. In a canonical C-II sequence, each exterior cap of area `Omega(D^2)` has a distinct cap with the same leading area and actual isolate population; the two actual isolate hulls have antipodal normalized limits. For each fixed `epsilon`, at most `pi/(4 epsilon)` caps have area at least `epsilon D^2`; the smaller caps contain at most `(sqrt(pi epsilon)/a_0)D^2+O(D)` exterior isolates, and every macroscopic cap has an opposite partner. This narrows the exterior-container branch to cap pairs, but does not give strict-safe capacity, a finite core release, or a source-preserving competitor. The internal inactive-container route remains separate. See the [independent Audit.115](../../audits/target_c_antipodal_exterior_cap_pairing_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.127 promotes C-OO-OVERLAP-AWARE-ROOT-RUN-CAPACITY. In the actual acute-binary pairwise-disjoint proper-cut system, each selected train has a unique zero/owner bank; loaded-hull runs and rhombus-only roots spend disjoint bank capacity, giving the bound by loaded-run count plus the rhombus-only owner count. The result closes the owner-to-train allocation gap recorded in Audit.63 for this precise system. It supplies no compatible source motion, unloaded-tail currency, or C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.127](../../audits/target_c_overlap_aware_owner_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

## Normal form and ZR/ZF scope

Use a contact-minimal, then diameter-pair-minimal triangle-free global minimizer and retain every source point, including contact-isolated points. Let `V` be the nonisolated positive-contact core and `v=|V|`.

The canonical §57 theorem is

\[
\ker\Omega_V=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1,P_x|_V,P_y|_V\},
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}[V]=2v-3,
\]

so `e+h>=2v-2` and `h>=delta>=ceil(D)` in the core ledger. This is not a full-source affine-kernel theorem. Contact-isolated coordinates add zero stress rows/columns and independent kernel directions. `C-ZRF-FULL-SOURCE` is rejected.

## Earlier detailed audit notes through audit.100

These notes are historical detail; the generated theorem manifest and summaries above control current claim authority through audit.108.

The generated theorem-state manifest controls exact claim status and scope; the summaries below record promoted results and their limits. Audits.52--96 remain in force only at their manifest-recorded scopes. Audit.61 reduces sufficiently small O--O finite-motion failure to
\[
\boxed{
\text{outer-face near-unit implication structure}
+\text{explicit unary rhombus blockers}
+\text{at most }d_H\text{ inactive upper exceptions}.
}
\]

Audit.62 adds two exact finite source-geometry restrictions on that remaining blocker structure.

### Loaded near-unit adjacency

Canonical \`C-LOADED-NEAR-UNIT-ADJACENCY\` is a universal finite statement. Let \(B\) be the set of actual diameter endpoints and assume \(\operatorname{conv}B\) has positive area. Put
\[
\tau_D=\frac{3D}{\sqrt{3D^2+4}+1}.
\]
If loaded endpoints \(a,b\in B\) are nonconsecutive on the loaded hull and \(|ab|<D\), then
\[
\boxed{|ab|\ge\tau_D.}
\]

For \(D\ge6\),
\[
\tau_D>\frac32>\frac{13}{12},
\]
so every loaded pair of length at most \(13/12\) is a side of the loaded hull. Therefore the loaded near-unit graph
\[
\{ab\subset B:\ |ab|\le13/12\}
\]
is a subgraph of the cyclic loaded hull and has maximum degree at most two.

This is stronger than merely saying loaded near-unit clauses are planar: their loaded endpoint incidence order is the actual loaded-hull order.

### Loaded-hull cap / attachment budget

Canonical \`C-LOADED-HULL-CAP-BUDGET\` applies to a finite unit-separated source \(A\) with \(K=\operatorname{conv}A\), cyclic loaded-hull vertex set \(B\), \(q=|B|\), \(F=\operatorname{conv}B\), and positive-contact core contained in \(F\). Put
\[
W=\operatorname{per}K-q.
\]

Then:

\[
\boxed{
\#\{\text{occupied loaded-hull caps}\}\le W,
\qquad
\sum_{\rm occupied}L_{ab}\le2W,
\qquad
\sum_{\rm occupied}|ab|\le2W.
}
\]

If \(P\) is the set of core points within distance \(<\sqrt2\) of a source point outside \(F\), then
\[
\boxed{|P|\le68W.}
\]

If \(X\) is the set of nonloaded source points in \(F\) within distance \(1/2\) of \(\partial F\), then
\[
\boxed{|X|\le12W.}
\]

Every source point \(p\in F\) with boundary distance at least \(1/2\) has the exact upper margin
\[
\boxed{|p-y|\le D-\frac12\qquad\forall y\in K.}
\]

Finally,
\[
\boxed{\#(\operatorname{ext}K\setminus B)\le W.}
\]

Canonical full-source geometry gives \(\operatorname{per}K<\pi D\). Hence along every canonical subsequence with
\[
q/D\to\pi,
\]
one has
\[
W=o(D),
\]
and therefore
\[
\boxed{
|P|=o(D),\qquad
|X|=o(D),\qquad
\#(\operatorname{ext}K\setminus B)=o(D).
}
\]

Thus hull-cap exterior geometry can support only a sublinear blocker population in the terminal \(q/D\to\pi\) regime.

### Audit.111: loaded-cap chord threshold and stress cut

`C-LOADED-EXTERIOR-CAP-CHORD-THRESHOLD` makes the cap base bound explicit. If a unit-separated finite source has diameter D, a chord ab of length ell<D has diameter partners on one side, and a source point lies on the other, then

\[
(1+D^{-2})\ell^2+2\ell/D\ge3,
\qquad
\ell\ge\tau_D=\frac{3D}{\sqrt{3D^2+4}+1}.
\]

For an occupied cap of the actual loaded hull, its source point is across the closing chord from the loaded endpoints' diameter partners. Thus the closing side is nonunit (indeed greater than 3/2 for D >= 6); this does not follow from the cap arc budget alone.

Audit.111 widens `C-LOADED-ONLY-STRESS-CYCLE-OBSTRUCTION` to every finite unit-separated source of diameter D >= 6 with a two-dimensional actual loaded hull and a normalized nonnegative equilibrium stress. Canonical `C-LOADED-NEAR-UNIT-ADJACENCY` places every loaded unit contact on the actual loaded-hull cycle. The virial identity and leaf-normal equilibrium then force any loaded-only stress to use the entire cycle, so any nonunit side makes that stress face empty. On the canonical D >= 9 C-II source, an occupied cap therefore empties the loaded-only stress face. Existing `C-UNLOADED-KKT-STRESS-TRANSVERSAL` then deletes at most s_Z+1 unloaded core points and gives a strict motion on the reduced-cardinality source while retaining contact isolates at their original coordinates.

This supplies no refill or same-cardinality competitor. The cap/chord and stress results narrow the Worker 2 E.4/E.5 route to restoring the deleted labels or proving an independent cardinality comparison; C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.111](../../audits/target_c_loaded_only_stress_scope_extension_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

### Audit.63 owner, terminal-train, and wall results

For every canonical full-core Type-II sequence with q/D -> pi and u_B=o(D), the complete stopped O--O train population is o(D), including singleton bridges. The exact face ledger gives 3x_B+5K_top+2X+S_BB <= [2(pi-s)+o(1)]D, where s=sqrt(pi/(2sqrt(3))); smaller linear banks remain possible.

In the narrow-window independent-binary owner scope, selected O--O trains are bounded by x_B+m_B+1; owner-region components obey K_own+rho_own <= T_OO; acute crossings are laminar; and distinct loaded binary witnesses number at most u_B. These statements do not cover general crossing multishear assignments.

For a convex actual unit-contact wall with one deleted vertex, strict clearance from the remaining wall vertices shields a replacement point from all exterior source points, including isolates. The one-deletion strict-safe region is empty for walls with at most five sides. In canonical physical unit hexagon faces, each interior point is covered by at least two closed unit disks centered at wall vertices. This is not a packet-capacity theorem. The two-deletion isolate counterexample remains binding.

Audit.128 promotes the exact finite regression C-TERMINAL-LONG-POCKET-ANCHOR-REGRESSION: a three-edge unit-contact hull pocket can have squared terminal-normal determinant 1/65, below the short-pocket threshold 1/4. Long pockets therefore require the separate u_B charge in the terminal shore argument. This local example has no actual diameter, KKT, or global-minimality claim and leaves the C-II endpoint unchanged.

Audit.129 promotes `C-ATOMIC-TRACK-BOUNDARY-COST`: every canonical full-source Type-II sequence satisfies `liminf (Q+4f+x_B+m)/D >= 4s_2`, giving `liminf (10(q-k)+2g-3z-u_B)/D >= 5pi+4s_2`. In the low-`t_D` range `t_D=(q-k)/D<=1.860+o(1)`, this forces `g=p-q >= (0.4586072477-o(1))D`, so `x_B=O(sqrt(D))` is impossible. Theorem A alone gives `q/D<=2.6829854059+o(1)`; the source verifier's stronger endpoint belongs to the separately audited Theorem B, not Theorem A. See the [independent Audit.129](../../audits/target_c_atomic_track_boundary_cost_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.130 promotes `C-ATOMIC-OUTER-ROUTE-EXPOSURE`: `liminf (Q+4f+x_B)/D >= 4s_2`, hence `liminf (10(q-k)+g-4z)/D >= 5pi+4s_2`. At low `t_D`, this forces `g-4z >= (0.9172144954-o(1))D` and `q/D<=2.2243781581850+o(1)`. The source verifier's endpoint arithmetic matches this result but does not verify its route-exposure proof.

Audit.131 promotes `C-ATOMIC-ONE-END-TRAIN-BOUNDARY`: `liminf (2f-k+m)/D >= 2s_2`, hence `liminf (5(q-k)+2g-z-u_B)/D >= 3pi+2s_2`. Thus `x_B=o(D)` forces `(q-k)/D>=2.2658807149-o(1)`. Combined with Audit.130, the low-`t_D` branch has `g/D>=1.0147017872-o(1)` and `q/D<=2.1268908664+o(1)`. These bounds remain compatible with the canonical lower bound on `q`; C-II is open. See the [independent Audit.131](../../audits/target_c_atomic_one_end_train_boundary_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.132 promotes the sharp per-orbit detour `lambda>=Phi(A)` for any finite positive direction measure with all projection-width integrals at least two. Its Type-II source consequence is `4s_2<=sqrt((pi+lambda)Phi^{-1}(lambda))+5(2t-pi-lambda)`, where `t=(q-k)/D` and `lambda=(b-p)/D` in a joint subsequential limit. This constrains the endpoint and exceptional-face budgets without forcing any particular orbit mass; the source bridge and C-II remain open. See the [independent Audit.132](../../audits/target_c_atomic_orbit_sharp_detour_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.133 promotes `C-RHOMBUS-SEPARATOR-CAPACITY` and `C-RHOMBUS-BULK-LOADED-RETURN-BOUND`: quadratic rhombus bulk in one unloaded component cannot be isolated from its actual loaded attachments by a sublinear vertex cut, and it yields `p>=ceil(F(R))` disjoint actual contact paths to distinct loaded endpoints. `C-CONVEX-FACE-LOADED-RETURN-BOUND` gives the stated linear path bound for a convex exceptional face. The audit also derives a deep-cap consequence under explicit cap/grain extraction premises, but that extraction is unresolved, so the consequence has no canonical theorem entry. The pinned-grid regression is confined to a pin-only framework and does not refute the actual-loaded source theorem. These paths still need to be charged to train/interface/face currency or used in a full-source strict-`D` replacement; they do not close C-II. See the [independent Audit.133](../../audits/target_c_rhombus_bulk_separator_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.134 promotes `C-FIXED-PORT-CONTACT-ROUTE-BOUND`: from a specified actual loaded attachment to any vertex of its unloaded component, an actual contact route has length at most `C R+C_0+10(e_BB-k)`, with `C=4/sqrt(3)+30pi` and `C_0=90pi+30`; its local form charges only augmented-face defects meeting the port segment and nearby attachments. `C-BOUNDED-FACE-PORT-ROUTE-BUDGET` shows that a long route at submacroscopic Euclidean distance requires a linear tail of high-degree augmented faces when the fixed degree cutoff is held constant. That tail is paid only once by the global Section-123 currency, so this gives no multi-route multiplicity bound and does not change the finite-move/payment obligation. The exact rational rhombus regression records that factor two is insufficient for a boundary detour; the audited factor `4/sqrt(3)` is retained. See the [independent Audit.134](../../audits/target_c_fixed_port_route_bound_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.136 records `C-TYPEII-NEAR-PORT-GRID-EXTRACTION` and `C-NEAR-PORT-DEFECT-TAIL-ALTERNATIVE` as conditional claims. Given the explicit same-cell macroscopic cap package in a canonical Type-II sequence, a quadratic coherent grid lies in every fixed small neighborhood of its loaded host port; uniformly vanishing degree tails give short port-to-grid access, while a persistent fixed-radius route obstruction forces `liminf T_M/D>=lambda/10` at each fixed cutoff. The cap-package extraction and tail hypothesis remain unproved; Section 123 permits a linear tail. This supplies no source move or multiplicity bound and does not change the C-II finite-move/payment obligation. See the [independent Audit.136](../../audits/target_c_typeii_near_port_grid_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.137 records `C-DEEP-FAR-COMPLETE-GRID-LOADED-RETURNS` as a conditional audited-working claim. From an explicit coherent quadratic grain inside a convex cap, trimming faces within `h=A_G/(8pi D)` of the cap boundary and above `H-s`, where `s=A_G/(4D)`, leaves a quadratic coherent component and a complete grid far from one actual loaded attachment. Its crossing rhombi yield `Omega(D)` vertex-disjoint actual paths to distinct loaded endpoints, with `Omega(D^2)` distinct internal vertices outside the grid. The cap/grain extraction and payment of this return packet remain unproved; no source move or C-II frontier reduction follows. See the [independent Audit.137](../../audits/target_c_deep_far_cap_grid_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.138 promotes the common-region theorem for support subtrees of pairwise alternating or endpoint-sharing boundary pairs in a proper disjoint-crosscut disk. Endpoint marks on crosscuts use all incident regions; a separate exact regression shows that common support does not by itself exclude a diameter pair from a nonhub branch. Audit.148 replaces the fourteen-cut tube estimate with a six-row Oler bound for short pairs in an explicitly supplied crosscut path. Audit.149 strengthens this to five rows for segment length at most `21/20`, using an exact finite capsule-packing certificate. Neither audit extracts such a path or provides a motion, so the finite-move/payment obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.138](../../audits/target_c_diameter_support_crosscut_hub_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md), [Audit.148](../../audits/target_c_oo_short_pair_path_bandwidth_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md), and [Audit.149](../../audits/target_c_five_row_capsule_packing_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.139 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-CAP-DIAMETER-SEPARATOR`, `C-EXTERIOR-CAP-CORE-CONTACT-CUT`, and `C-EXTERIOR-CAP-FULL-SOURCE-STRIP-DESCENT`. A fixed-area actual exterior cap puts all actual diameter edges across a line, forces the Woodall forest branch, and excludes `q/D -> pi`; the cap cut yields a strict sub-`D` source after deleting at most `(2/sqrt(3)+1)D+2` unloaded core labels while retaining every contact isolate. The full-source strip variant deletes at most `(2a/(sqrt(3)/2)+1)D+2a+1` labels and uses a permitted translation `0<epsilon<a-1/2`. The nonempty core cut is established by a same-cardinality contradiction if there were no crossing contact; contact-graph connectivity is not assumed. Both results stop at reduced cardinality and supply no refill packet, so the C-II finite-move/payment obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.139](../../audits/target_c_exterior_cap_separator_motion_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.140 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-INTERNAL-WALL-ONE-GUARD-CAPACITY`. In any canonical hull-augmented cell, if v is an internal vertex of the actual unit-contact path, the strict unit-separated packet capacity remains exactly `|S_Q|` even when the guard at v is omitted. The one-cell, one-extra-site packet proposed in the exterior-cap report is therefore impossible in a canonical source. The qualifying-vertex extraction from every occupied macroscopic cap cell, moved-core refill, and multi-cell compatibility are not established; the C-II finite-move/payment obligation remains open. See the [independent Audit.140](../../audits/target_c_exterior_internal_wall_vertex_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.141 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-CELL-WEAK-GUARD-LEDGER`. Under weak guards at every path vertex but one internal vertex, the full-source shield keeps all retained lower distances feasible and makes every new contact local to the packet or a retained wall vertex. The exact ledger counts all such contacts and excludes an equal-cardinality replacement when it covers all old diameter pairs, or when the full contact/diameter-pair endpoint hypotheses hold. No packet is constructed, triangle-freeness is still required for the lexicographic branch, and moved-wall or multi-cell refill remains open. See the [independent Audit.141](../../audits/target_c_exterior_cell_weak_guard_contact_ledger_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.142 records `C-RHOMBUS-EDGE-COMPLETION-COLLISION-REGRESSION`: in a strict unit rhombus, the same-side equilateral completion of one side can lie inside the rhombus yet collide with its opposite vertex. This refutes the local edge-completion-as-vacancy inference only; the exact four-point example is nonglobal and does not change the exterior-cell ledger or C-II frontier. See the [independent Audit.142](../../audits/target_c_rhombus_edge_completion_collision_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.143 records `C-PARTIAL-WALL-INTERIOR-CHORD-ISOLATE-REGRESSION`: a nonunit side of an apparent partial wall may be an interior chord of the actual source hull, with a retained contact isolate beyond it. A proposed point can pass every remaining unit-path vertex guard but lie below unit distance from that isolate. Treat such labels as cell population; do not use an artificial chord as the hull arc. This reinforces the existing hull-augmented-cell hypothesis and does not change C-II. See the [independent Audit.143](../../audits/target_c_partial_hull_interior_chord_isolate_regression_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.144 promotes `C-FINITE-SORTING-PACKET-RADIAL-LIFTING-STRESS` for exactly 33 specified sorting packets. The radial-height lift produces a positive pinned stress on every moving-involving contact in those packets. All cases have a unit triangle, and the finite list gives no all-`M` or all-order stress theorem. This does not change C-II. See the [independent Audit.144](../../audits/target_c_finite_radial_lifting_stress_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.145 promotes `C-STRICT-TURN-PATH-CONTACT-NON-SURROUNDING`: contacts from one site to a convex unit path of total turn below pi cannot strictly surround the site when all internal path vertices are weakly guarded and no path edge has both endpoints at unit distance from the site. The one-site opening consequence still requires strict margins to all other source points. This gives no packet or C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.145](../../audits/target_c_strict_turn_path_non_surrounding_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.148 promotes `C-OO-SHORT-PAIR-PATH-BANDWIDTH`. For a supplied ordered path of proper disjoint crosscuts whose intervening rows are crossed by distinct actual strict unit-rhombus centerlines, finite Oler packing bounds the row span by `1+(14L+5pi)/(3sqrt(3))`; if `L<=1+K/D` and `D>10K`, the integer span is at most six. This is a conditional path-geometry estimate: it gives no extraction of the path, finite motion, or strict-diameter competitor. C-II is unchanged. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_oo_short_pair_path_bandwidth_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.149 promotes `C-UNIT-CAPSULE-PACKING-5` and `C-OO-SHORT-PAIR-PATH-BANDWIDTH-5`: every `sqrt(3)/2`-separated set in the radius-`1/2` capsule of a segment of length at most `21/20` has at most five points; an exact rational five-point packing at length one proves sharpness. Consequently the supplied crosscut path has span at most five in that segment-length range, hence for `L<=1+K/D` when `D>=20K`. The general Oler bound remains available for longer segments. The finite certificate does not verify a path extraction, blocker ownership, exact-swap density, Horn propagation, or source motion. C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.149](../../audits/target_c_five_row_capsule_packing_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.150 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-CAP-LOCAL-STRESS-TRANSVERSALS`: each interior parallel cut supplied by the actual exterior-cap separator has aggregate crossing-contact stress projection at least `2g`, and a set of at most `s_Z+1` unloaded core vertices hits the positive contact support of every normalized stress. Two-unit-separated cuts give disjoint transversals, with `Omega(D)` choices for a fixed-area cap. The canonical consequence is a family of separate reduced-source descents; no simultaneous motion or refill follows. The full-source refill/capacity obligation and C-II frontier are unchanged. See the [independent Audit.150](../../audits/target_c_exterior_cap_local_stress_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.152 promotes `C-CENTERED-FAN-BLOCKER-DEPTH-PATH`. Each supplied degree-two actual-diameter fan leaf in the canonical full-source normalization has a directed lower-blocker path to support depth `D/10`; the path has at least `D/11` near-unit edges and total edge-gap excess below `1/10+1/D+1/D^2`. If the path does not reach another nonisolated vertex first, it contains at least `D/11-1` contact isolates. This is a per-leaf result: it gives no path disjointness, canonical face charge, or source competitor, so the C-II finite-move/payment obligation is unchanged. The source report's face-localization extension remains conditional. See the [independent Audit.152](../../audits/target_c_centered_fan_blocker_closure_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.153 promotes `C-ROOTED-CROSSCUT-MARKED-INTERVAL-CAPACITY`: for any root of a finite dual tree of pairwise disjoint proper crosscuts, marks assigned to a component after root deletion form a consecutive boundary interval, so at most two consecutive marked-cycle pairs cross it. In the canonical O--O use, every loaded-loaded lower pair of length at most `13/12` is a loaded-hull side; the resulting two-per-branch clause bound requires the same fixed single-region assignment used by the motion model. The broader claim that every nonhub branch is free of a complete diameter pair is false when a pair's marks are crosscut endpoints, as recorded by Audit.138. The active C-II obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.153](../../audits/target_c_rooted_crosscut_branch_interval_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.154 promotes `C-OO-SINGLETON-BAD-TERMINAL-CAPACITY`: any selected family of singleton O--O bridge trains injects into the canonical bad outer-terminal bank and has size at most `x_B+m_B`. The proposed dual-tree clause-range extension still depends on the unproved fourteen-cut estimate withheld by Audit.138; the valid singleton count gives no bound on non-singleton path length, so the C-II obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.154](../../audits/target_c_singleton_oo_clause_range_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.155 refines `C-CONTACT-BUDGET-DIAMETER-EXCHANGE-ENDPOINT`: the `j<t` case needs only contact minimality and permits new-involving pairs at distance `D`; the `j=t,d>0` case retains the secondary diameter-pair minimum and strict upper slack. The one-spare-site exclusion follows by deleting a nonisolated contact vertex and inserting only strictly separated sites; this adds no packet capacity or source extraction. See the [independent scope audit](../../audits/target_c_one_spare_site_scope_refinement_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.157 records `C-TYPEII-CAP-LOCAL-HARMONIC-GRID` as audited-working under the explicit premise that the convex hull of actual loaded diameter endpoints has area at least `alpha D^2`. In canonical Type II, that cap then contains a quadratic coherent rhombus subgrain and a linear-by-linear phase-pure grid inside one unloaded harmonic cell. The cap extraction from arbitrary sources is not established, and no source move or payment follows; the C-II frontier is unchanged. See the [independent cap-localization audit](../../audits/target_c_cap_local_harmonic_grid_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.158 records `C-TYPEII-CAP-DEEP-SAME-HOST-ATTACHMENT` as audited-working when that quadratic-area loaded-endpoint cap lies in a proper boundary interval and contains the cap-local harmonic grain. It forces an actual loaded attachment on the same interval at height `Omega(D)`, with an actual unit contact into the grain's unloaded component, and a linear arc-length excess over the endpoint chord. This supplies the explicit attachment premise of Audit.136 under the same cap hypotheses; the cap/interval extraction, route from the port to the grid, currency payment, and finite-move obligation remain open. There is no C-II frontier closure. See the [independent attachment audit](../../audits/target_c_deep_same_host_attachment_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.160 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-OCCUPIED-CAP-INTERNAL-WALL-VERTEX`. Every occupied actual exterior cap has an internal actual-contact vertex on its hull-augmented cell boundary path, because the canonical cap-chord bound makes the distinct base endpoints nonunit. Together with Audit.140, this rules out the one-extra-site strict packet confined to that fixed cell. The fixed-cell route is closed for occupied caps; moved-wall refill, cross-cell compatibility, exact-contact packets, and cap extraction remain open. C-II is unchanged. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_occupied_cap_internal_wall_vertex_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.161 promotes `C-UNIT-WALL-DELAUNAY-DEFECT-BUDGET` and `C-UNIT-WALL-ONE-DEFICIT-DICHOTOMY` as auxiliary fixed-container results. A maximal completion inside any convex unit-edge wall yields the explicit Delaunay excess and exceptional-set bounds; at one-deficit slack the alternatives are a canonical low-slack chart after one addition or the `51 / 137 / 541` defect bounds without an addition. Diffuse metric strain may remain outside the exceptional set, and neither branch supplies an original-source vacancy or move. This does not change the C-II source-move/payment obligation. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_one_deficit_delaunay_defect_budget_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.162 promotes `C-FIVE-POINT-HULL-PERIMETER-LOWER-BOUND` and `C-UNIT-PENTAGON-PERIMETER-CAPACITY`. Any five mutually unit-separated points have convex-hull perimeter at least five; consequently the only five-point unit-separated subset of a strictly convex unit-sided pentagon is its vertex set. Deleting d>=1 vertices permits at most d-1 globally separated strict-interior replacement points clear of the retained vertices, and retaining all five permits none. A vertex-disjoint bank obeys the summed deficit under disjoint label assignment. This is a fixed-container count, not a full-source packet or diameter-improvement theorem; C-II remains open. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_unit_pentagon_perimeter_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

### Audit.64 finite packet endpoints

For a contact-minimal full-source representative, let t count all old contacts removed by R, d the old actual diameter pairs removed, and j every new unit pair touching an inserted point. If j<t, contact minimality alone excludes the packet whenever it remains triangle-free, unit-separated, and has diameter at most D; new pairs may lie at D. If j=t and d>0, the same conclusion uses secondary diameter-pair minimality and strict upper slack for every new-involving pair. This uses the full source, including retained isolates. A strictly separated packet with one spare site is a corollary after deleting any nonisolated contact vertex.

A finite packet with at most two new unit contacts and strict upper slack can open all new contacts by an arbitrarily small perturbation of inserted points. A one-site packet without such an opening direction must have at least three retained unit neighbors whose displacement vectors strictly surround the site. These lemmas constrain candidate packets; they do not establish that a protected packet exists.

### Audit.65 regular-shell wall barrier

In a regular odd unit `q`-gon (`q>=65`), one strict interior contact isolate can block at most two adjacent inward one-vertex proposals. Thus blocking all proposals costs at least `(q+1)/2` distinct isolates in a fixed radial collar. A matching/singleton collar cover and deep lattice filling realize the barrier, but the resulting quadratic source is not globally optimal: an independent whole-source packet of the same cardinality has strictly smaller diameter. The result sharpens the one-vertex wall-shield guardrail without bounding capacity in arbitrary macroscopic containers.

### Audit.66 collective wall deletion

For a convex actual unit-contact wall, a deleted vertex set `B` with pairwise distances at least two has a collective exterior shield: a replacement point strictly clear of the remaining wall is strictly separated from every retained exterior source point, including isolates. The `M` wall vertices of a unit-separated source contain at least `ceil(M/25)` such deletions. If a unit-separated packet of size `|A intersect int(F)|+|B|` fits in this region, Audit.64's generic finite endpoint rules it out by losing at least `2|B|` contacts. The exact D(8) heptagon-plus-center source shows the region can have capacity only one even at a global minimizer; no large-source capacity theorem follows.

## Exact remaining split

### Full-core branch

The audit.61 finite-motion obstruction remains
\[
\text{outer-face near-unit owner/implication structure}
+\text{unary rhombus blockers}
+\text{at most }d_H\text{ upper exceptions},
\]
but audit.62 removes two large sources of ambiguity and audit.63 rules out a linear O--O train bank in the q/D -> pi, u_B=o(D) regime.

1. **Loaded-loaded lower clauses are cyclically ordered.** Any loaded pair in the audit.61 near-unit range is forced onto the loaded hull cycle; each loaded vertex supports at most two such loaded neighbors.

2. **Terminal cap/exterior blockers are sublinear.** Along \(q/D\to\pi\), exterior extreme isolates, short core-to-outside attachments through loaded-hull caps, and the shallow nonloaded loaded-hull boundary layer are all \(o(D)\). Deep core points have an exact \(D-1/2\) inactive-upper margin.

Therefore any *linear* family of lower blockers surviving in the terminal regime must concentrate in one of the following:

- actual outer-contact-face geometry inside the loaded hull;
- the cyclic loaded-hull near-unit graph;
- explicitly retained unary rhombus blockers;
- the at most \(d_H\) inactive upper exceptions.

The highest-value next theorem is a source-realized **face-coupled finite-move theorem**: from the remaining outer-to-bounded or bounded-to-bounded train system, construct a finite move or discrete replacement that passes every source lower constraint, actual-diameter upper constraint, and unary rhombus test, then yields strict contact loss at diameter D or a strict sub-D competitor. Owner-order arguments should serve this endpoint directly.

A source-level path forest remains insufficient after owner contraction.

### Macroscopic-container branch

Audit.63 adds a one-deleted-vertex shield; Audit.66 extends it to pairwise-separated deletion sets. Audit.65 quantifies the collar isolate population needed to block every regular-shell proposal and gives a strictly nonglobal blocked filling. The D(8) capacity regression shows these shields need not yield a replacement slot for small sources. Audit.89 forces an area-`Omega(D^2)` bounded physical face in the subquadratic-core branch to have at least half its boundary within every fixed macroscopic scale of actual loaded endpoints. The bound applies only to actual bounded contact faces; outer pockets and exterior caps remain separate, and the theorem supplies no packet capacity. No result establishes packet capacity for arbitrary large containers or closes the separate macroscopic inactive-container route. Audit.59's strict-safe area no-go remains binding.

## Binding rejected routes

Do not use without a new proof:

- incident-diameter sequence `=>` Euler trail/path;
- FB4 force-bulk localization;
- incompatible-cut C53 pentagon objection;
- RSR disk-equality uniqueness;
- Eppstein near-equality `=>` near quadrangulation;
- five-host occupancy from angular-measure saturation;
- inward OP pocket `subset` exterior Reuleaux minor cap;
- deletion of contact-isolated source points;
- full-source extension of the §57 affine-kernel theorem;
- `r/(6C_T)` as the sharp Section-90 bin-method limit;
- the withdrawn §121 normalization `G_loc=o(D^2) => G_loc/d=o(1)`.

The PR #191 `0.995540...` theorem remains rejected. The finite S1 `q=13` object remains a regression, not a global-minimizer theorem.
